121
00:08:33,340 --> 00:08:34,460
I'm sure it's on the news.

122
00:08:34,460 --> 00:08:42,340
But yeah, you land in that airport in Zzajau which is not spelled like Zzajau sounds.

123
00:08:42,340 --> 00:08:47,260
You land there and there's like Patriot missiles and all this anti-air stuff and you're like,

124
00:08:47,260 --> 00:08:49,580
my landing in a war zone?

125
00:08:49,580 --> 00:08:50,580
And you sort of are.

126
00:08:50,580 --> 00:08:54,760
So yeah, that was really an interesting experience and a good eye opener.

127
00:08:54,760 --> 00:08:59,180
So like you are leaving the safe zone.

128
00:08:59,180 --> 00:09:04,060
And then I think I ran into Sarah, I can't remember if I ran into you there in Poland

129
00:09:04,060 --> 00:09:05,780
or in Ukraine.

130
00:09:05,780 --> 00:09:06,780
It's sort of all.

131
00:09:06,780 --> 00:09:12,100
Yeah, we met in Poland, grabbed the ambulance and then we all went over the next day.

132
00:09:12,100 --> 00:09:13,100
Oh, that's right.

133
00:09:13,100 --> 00:09:14,100
That's right.

134
00:09:14,100 --> 00:09:15,100
Yeah.

135
00:09:15,100 --> 00:09:16,100
Thank goodness someone doesn't have Alzheimer's.

136
00:09:16,100 --> 00:09:23,860
And yeah, so that's how I ended up with the GRM and I ended up staying there for a month.

137
00:09:23,860 --> 00:09:26,980
What exactly was the project you guys were doing?

138
00:09:27,180 --> 00:09:32,260
I know, Jirim had like a more general mission, but what was the specific aspect that you

139
00:09:32,260 --> 00:09:38,260
guys were working on?

140
00:09:38,260 --> 00:09:41,000
So we were doing medical evacuation.

141
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:44,820
One of the, especially early on, one of the few things that the Ministry of Health had

142
00:09:44,820 --> 00:09:53,020
actually asked for support with was bringing patients from Lviv to Zzajau, mostly because

143
00:09:53,140 --> 00:09:59,280
the majority of their crews couldn't cross the border because the majority of their medics

144
00:09:59,280 --> 00:10:02,940
and their techs are fighting age men.

145
00:10:02,940 --> 00:10:08,740
So they had a very, very, very small number of personnel who could drive patients to these

146
00:10:08,740 --> 00:10:10,420
evacuation planes.

147
00:10:10,420 --> 00:10:13,540
And the planes had capacity of about 30 patients each.

148
00:10:13,540 --> 00:10:15,540
So these would be huge convoys.

149
00:10:15,540 --> 00:10:21,940
And actually, even later on in the conflict, when they did figure out being able to get

150
00:10:22,180 --> 00:10:29,180
fighting age men to be able to cross the border in that context, it still was a huge pull

151
00:10:29,180 --> 00:10:35,740
on their resources for a city to be able to provide like 30 ambulances in the same day

152
00:10:36,020 --> 00:10:37,020
for an entire day.

153
00:10:37,980 --> 00:10:46,540
So one of the things they asked for support from international NGOs and also from some

154
00:10:46,540 --> 00:10:53,420
of the other countries in the EU was just to drive patients on that transport.

155
00:10:53,980 --> 00:10:59,740
And that was about a, it took about six hours, including messing around at the border.

156
00:11:01,300 --> 00:11:06,060
Right on. So it was the MOH as a Ministry of Health in Ukraine.

157
00:11:06,460 --> 00:11:14,340
They were having you guys help ambulance patients from Lviv, which is a city in Ukraine

158
00:11:14,340 --> 00:11:15,780
across the border into Poland.

159
00:11:15,780 --> 00:11:17,700
And then they would get flown out from there.

