00:11:18,020 --> 00:11:19,420
And where were they getting flown out to?

161
00:11:19,900 --> 00:11:21,700
How did that side of the operation look?

162
00:11:22,940 --> 00:11:24,260
Predominantly Germany.

163
00:11:25,060 --> 00:11:29,540
But so the really big transport plane came from Germany.

164
00:11:30,100 --> 00:11:35,020
But there were smaller planes going through a lot of other countries in the EU.

165
00:11:36,580 --> 00:11:39,820
There's this whole EU Medivac system.

166
00:11:40,140 --> 00:11:43,980
So a lot of countries in the European Union were accepting patients.

167
00:11:44,940 --> 00:11:48,100
Primarily patients with traumatic injuries, but also

168
00:11:51,700 --> 00:11:56,620
and it was often people requiring rehabilitation or requiring prosthesis.

169
00:11:58,380 --> 00:12:03,500
Or another big one was patients with limbs and external fixation

170
00:12:05,100 --> 00:12:08,260
because they pretty much don't do internal fixation in Ukraine.

171
00:12:08,460 --> 00:12:13,580
So once the patients were brought out, then they would have additional surgery and get

172
00:12:14,180 --> 00:12:16,900
internal fixation in another country in the EU.

173
00:12:18,460 --> 00:12:23,220
Right on. And most of the patients you were transporting, at least the ones with

174
00:12:23,220 --> 00:12:25,740
traumatic injuries, those were related to the conflict, I assume.

175
00:12:27,260 --> 00:12:28,780
Yeah, all conflict related.

176
00:12:29,660 --> 00:12:33,620
Awesome. And then Jason, what kind of capabilities do you guys have on the

177
00:12:34,500 --> 00:12:36,420
what did it look like when you were actually doing the transfers?

178
00:12:37,700 --> 00:12:43,180
Well, we we'll be at the two ambulances, the one that we purchased there in Poland

179
00:12:43,180 --> 00:12:46,940
and then the one that was donated by the Finnish, I believe, Finland.

180
00:12:47,340 --> 00:12:49,500
And what were those ambulance names?

181
00:12:51,540 --> 00:12:53,220
Let's see. One was Homer.

182
00:12:53,220 --> 00:12:54,540
That was the big yellow one.

183
00:12:54,540 --> 00:12:57,540
And the other was Vinny.

184
00:12:57,980 --> 00:12:59,980
That's strong. Very strong names.

185
00:13:01,340 --> 00:13:02,940
Really glad to remember the names.

186
00:13:04,980 --> 00:13:08,900
So the smaller ambulance, you know, both had oxygen.

187
00:13:09,380 --> 00:13:15,980
We had one ventilator, lots of medicines, lots of

188
00:13:17,060 --> 00:13:18,220
donated medicines.

189
00:13:18,220 --> 00:13:19,580
So it was all types of stuff.

190
00:13:19,580 --> 00:13:21,140
We had to sort of look up the names.

191
00:13:22,380 --> 00:13:25,660
We we had a lot enough for, you know, running a couple.

192
00:13:27,700 --> 00:13:28,580
Couple of codes.

193
00:13:28,580 --> 00:13:33,300
We had quite a bit of medicine and equipment where we could have done really

194
00:13:33,300 --> 00:13:34,300
critical patients.

195
00:13:34,780 --> 00:13:38,820
But the issue is, if you look on a map, it I wasn't until I was there,

196
00:13:38,820 --> 00:13:41,260
if you're really humbled is how big Ukraine is.

197
00:13:41,300 --> 00:13:45,060
Right. So to drive from one side to the other would take

198
00:13:46,540 --> 00:13:48,500
18 hours. I'm just pulling that number out.

199
00:13:48,500 --> 00:13:50,220
But it's a it's a huge country.

200
00:13:51,940 --> 00:13:55,380
So, yes, we did have capabilities to do some crazy stuff.

201
00:13:55,820 --> 00:14:00,180
But if you had to do a long transport, you really had to factor in your

202
00:14:00,940 --> 00:14:07,180
your medical equipment that you had oxygen as well as any type of drips

203
00:14:07,180 --> 00:14:11,140
or anything you had, because what I found out later was, you know, here in

204
00:14:11,140 --> 00:14:14,140
the US, if you're doing a transport and you're running into trouble, you know,

205
00:14:14,140 --> 00:14:16,980
you have an airway issue or you're running law and medicine, you can

