436
00:29:40,660 --> 00:29:43,660
I think that's where we ran into an issue that I wish I would have seen fixed.

437
00:29:44,660 --> 00:29:46,660
And I'm sure they have ways of fixing it now.

438
00:29:47,660 --> 00:29:54,660
But during transport, one of the most important thing I feel we do is we get a good report and we hand off that information to the patient.

439
00:29:55,660 --> 00:30:10,660
And we have a good receiving facility. And when somebody is speaking, say French to me, and then I have to give that French report to a Ukrainian doctor, like this important things like in that case, he was like, why exactly was this patient intubated?

440
00:30:11,660 --> 00:30:14,660
And I was like, yes, and I pointed down.

441
00:30:15,660 --> 00:30:22,660
There was more to it. And that's very, very important because he's going through the paperwork and he may not understand what exactly happened.

442
00:30:22,660 --> 00:30:35,660
So that's a tough thing. So the solution I came up with and I never got to implement it was to actually just have the people record on my phone, a head to toe report.

443
00:30:36,660 --> 00:30:43,660
I'll get as much as I can out of it, whatever I need for the transport. And then when I arrive, I can either WhatsApp or play it for that report.

444
00:30:44,660 --> 00:30:46,660
So that way I'm just sort of a hander of his report.

445
00:30:46,660 --> 00:30:57,660
So but yeah, that train report or the train situation was a really cool experience other than, you know, we have a bunch of injured people in a train with a whole bunch of ambulances.

446
00:30:58,660 --> 00:31:04,660
I'm like, you know, is Putin going to accidentally shoot one of those rockets this way? So but never did. So it worked out well.

447
00:31:05,660 --> 00:31:22,660
And does it does seem I remember feeling that it was like kind of a like a risky endeavor, like a train is a very predictable target and we're essentially moving like a hospital on a train track like back and forth across the country.

448
00:31:23,660 --> 00:31:40,660
But I guess that would be like a very like high visibility strike and it would garner a lot of attention. So I think that was probably a contributing factor to on the safety side of the operation is that everyone was like disincentivized to make strikes against that kind of thing.

449
00:31:41,660 --> 00:31:42,660
At least at the time when we were there.

450
00:31:43,660 --> 00:32:03,660
Yeah, I think a lot of people were back in sort of April, May, a lot of people were expecting that with the rail infrastructure targeting, even if the train itself wasn't directly targeted that that Ukraine just wouldn't be able to keep open the rail infrastructure with the amount that it was getting hit.

451
00:32:03,660 --> 00:32:13,660
So I think it's I think a lot of people are kind of pleasantly surprised that that was still able to run for this long. And also just the normal rail services are still able to run for this long.

452
00:32:15,660 --> 00:32:30,660
Yeah, I mean, I was pretty impressed when I was over there that this has been quite a while. So I imagine things may be different now. But just the fact that so much infrastructure was still intact like, you know, self service was, you know, just as reliable over there as it is here in home.

453
00:32:30,660 --> 00:32:44,660
Although arguably, I mean, New Mexico, it's not always that great. But like, self service was fine. Like, riding the train was super easy, like getting around at that point was like, yeah, pretty easy, pretty manageable.

454
00:32:45,660 --> 00:32:57,660
Yeah, I mean, I'd say definitely still similar. Now, there's going further east of Nipro, you start running into challenges with some of those things. Self service starts becoming spotty or non existent.

455
00:32:58,660 --> 00:33:18,660
And there's only trains, I think, as far east as Kramatorsk now, especially getting around access to the newly liberated areas that can be quite challenging. It's been okay for us so far. But I think it's a lot easier to get through checkpoints and ambulances.

456
00:33:19,660 --> 00:33:26,660
For sure, for sure. Yeah, they put them all tough cocktails down when they see license sirens. Just sort of nice.

457
00:33:28,660 --> 00:33:36,660
Yeah, and I think that's relatively universal, like around the world is that you get a little bit of more leeway with things when you're in a vehicle with lights and sirens.

458
00:33:37,660 --> 00:33:52,660
Yeah, I actually was under the impression that Ukrainians were just incredibly courteous drivers until I spent about six weeks working on another one of our programs and we just had a van and a rental car.

459
00:33:53,660 --> 00:33:57,660
And I was like, wait, Ukrainians are not courteous drivers, Ukrainians just yield to ambulances.

460
00:33:58,660 --> 00:34:16,660
Going back to your idea about the train, I know that Kaddis had this idea about the big bus or the ICU bus kind of to fill a gap for transporting like larger quantities of patience back and forth.

461
00:34:17,660 --> 00:34:25,660
Are there other folks doing something similar or is it essentially just the big bus that you guys have, the ICU bus and the train?

462
00:34:26,660 --> 00:34:32,660
What are the available options for moving patients around in more than one-off situations like you do in an ambulance?

463
00:34:33,660 --> 00:34:41,660
So there's also a series of buses that have come or are in the process of coming from Norway.

464
00:34:42,660 --> 00:34:50,660
I think they are ultimately going to end up delivering six buses to Ukraine that will be based in various cities.

465
00:34:53,660 --> 00:34:58,660
I couldn't tell you how many of those have already arrived. I know there's one currently working out of Lviv.

466
00:34:59,660 --> 00:35:14,660
But I guess the Norwegians kind of had the same thought that we did, that there was potentially going to be a little bit of gap in regards to the train if rail infrastructure continued to be targeted.

467
00:35:15,660 --> 00:35:28,660
And so there was this decision to provide a lot of buses. I think that I would say that the train is probably still the primary way that patients get moved long distance.

468
00:35:29,660 --> 00:35:47,660
There's also a number of other, and I can't really speak to their capacities, but I know that in addition to the MSF train, there are other patient transport trains, some of which I think are being run by the government.

469
00:35:48,660 --> 00:36:03,660
I don't know what kind of capacities for patient care they have on them, but they exist as well. I mean, rail infrastructure is kind of just, I guess, the best way to be moving people or things in a country this size when you can't do air travel.

470
00:36:04,660 --> 00:36:14,660
Right. Yeah, I know there was also concerns when I was over there about the fuel situation, which made it a little bit problematic with vehicles.

471
00:36:15,660 --> 00:36:24,660
Yeah, the fuel situation got a lot better to the point that it was really no different from trying to buy fuel anywhere else.

472
00:36:25,660 --> 00:36:42,660
I haven't had a problem buying fuel at all up until last week, like two days ago, and I found myself getting shut down at like four gas stations in a row trying to buy diesel and then eventually got just 20 litres out of one of them.

473
00:36:43,660 --> 00:36:46,660
And I think we only got 20 litres because we were an ambulance.

474
00:36:47,660 --> 00:36:57,660
But I don't know whether that was just kind of a weird blip on that area on that day, or whether that's a sign of something more difficult and challenging to come.

475
00:36:58,660 --> 00:37:02,660
Right on. Well, hopefully you can still find fuel or you have some stockpiled somewhere.

476
00:37:05,660 --> 00:37:11,660
Yeah, I mean, it's been pretty easy in Nipro. We were further out east when we were having those problems.

477
00:37:12,660 --> 00:37:25,660
Nice. And then at the moment, like with your role there, are you are you involved in direct patient care? Are you more helping out with like the admin side of things and the organisational level?

478
00:37:26,660 --> 00:37:40,660
Admin and organisational things, really. So I kind of I'm kind of overseeing everything that we're doing on the ground here. So the Medivac portion to an extent, I think, is a really good thing.

479
00:37:41,660 --> 00:37:46,660
And also the training programme. We're also doing a lot of equipment distribution at the moment.

480
00:37:47,660 --> 00:38:03,660
So providing things like ambulances and ventilators to various local groups, local NGOs, local EMS services that are that, you know, have a need.

481
00:38:04,660 --> 00:38:13,660
So I'm kind of just overseeing all of those different threads right now. It's pretty interesting, actually. I've kind of got my hand in a lot of pies.

482
00:38:14,660 --> 00:38:24,660
Nice. And are you I know with GRM, you were sort of managing volunteers, a germ of like bring volunteers over and you kind of put them to work. Are you doing something similar with caddis?

483
00:38:25,660 --> 00:38:38,660
Yeah, right now we only actually have the one volunteer because we are sort of because we're slowing down what we're doing with Medivac to focus more on the training and the distribution.

484
00:38:39,660 --> 00:38:49,660
And then there are a few other staff members out here that are also paramedics. So we can handle calls that we get just by kind of pulling them away from their other obligations.

485
00:38:50,660 --> 00:39:00,660
But we think that we are actually a little bit busy to maintain that. So we might scale up volunteers again.

486
00:39:01,660 --> 00:39:09,660
But generally, up to about now, our programmes have been have been run through working with volunteers the same as they were at GRM.

487
00:39:10,660 --> 00:39:14,660
Right on. Well, maybe you can recruit Jason to get back over there after his next work hitch.

488
00:39:15,660 --> 00:39:22,660
Well, yeah, I'll speak to some of the volunteer greatness that happened when I I volunteered in lots of places around the world.

489
00:39:23,660 --> 00:39:33,660
And a lot of the times you have to pay for your own flight there, pay for your own place to stay, meals and all that. And I mean, I felt like a king working for GRM.

490
00:39:34,660 --> 00:39:45,660
They actually took care of the flights and I believe they use that company Airlink, which is probably one of the unsung euros of this whole medical response to Ukraine.

491
00:39:46,660 --> 00:39:55,660
They do advertise and tooth or own horn on occasion, but really, they're they're finding ways to get people over there. So the companies don't have to pay.

492
00:39:56,660 --> 00:40:04,660
And, you know, they gave us a little bit of money for food every day, which is more than enough for what we needed and gave us a place to sleep every night.

493
00:40:05,660 --> 00:40:14,660
So that was very nice. You know, we didn't have to sleep in the ambulance. So of all the place I volunteered, it was one of the nicest, you know, and other than the occasional air raid alarm.

494
00:40:15,660 --> 00:40:17,660
And you guys had a pretty cool apartment there in the D's.

495
00:40:18,660 --> 00:40:25,660
You know what? I actually still have I rented that same apartment again on behalf of I liked it.

496
00:40:26,660 --> 00:40:28,660
That's awesome. Great. It was really great.

497
00:40:30,660 --> 00:40:32,660
Yeah, that was definitely a super cool spot.

498
00:40:35,660 --> 00:40:41,660
Okay, so the most important question I have for you, Sarah, is when when are you going to go to medical school?

499
00:40:42,660 --> 00:40:43,660
I was waiting for that one.

500
00:40:44,660 --> 00:40:45,660
What's the game plan?

501
00:40:46,660 --> 00:40:51,660
It's not it's not something that's I'm specifically looking to do at this point in time.

502
00:40:52,660 --> 00:40:56,660
Wait, I can't hear you. I think you said that you're not looking to do it.

503
00:40:58,660 --> 00:41:07,660
Right now, I think my long term goals are sort of sticking around with humanitarianism, doing project management, things like that.

504
00:41:07,660 --> 00:41:13,660
However, you probably could argue that I've got a little bit of the medical bug now.

505
00:41:14,660 --> 00:41:18,660
So I guess come back and ask me in two years and we'll see.

506
00:41:19,660 --> 00:41:20,660
All right. Perfect.

507
00:41:22,660 --> 00:41:25,660
Well, I think those are all the questions that I had specifically for you guys.

508
00:41:26,660 --> 00:41:34,660
I don't know if either of you had anything else you wanted to mention about the experience over there in Ukraine or just like working abroad in this capacity in general.

509
00:41:34,660 --> 00:41:37,660
August, I guess I'll just open up to you guys.

510
00:41:40,660 --> 00:41:45,660
I'll go first. I think it was it was a positive experience.

511
00:41:47,660 --> 00:41:53,660
Maybe too much so because I don't I don't even talk about this experience with anyone.

512
00:41:56,660 --> 00:41:59,660
Yeah, it just really it really touched me.

513
00:42:00,660 --> 00:42:01,660
I mean, yeah, so

514
00:42:01,660 --> 00:42:07,660
they had a great leadership over there, believe it or not. It was an organization. I think GRM and

515
00:42:09,660 --> 00:42:19,660
you know, you guys saw a lot more behind the scenes. But for the for the grunt on the ground, which is what I was, we had really good leadership there. So good job, Sarah.

516
00:42:20,660 --> 00:42:23,660
You did a great job. And you're really

517
00:42:24,660 --> 00:42:30,660
you're really obviously from the from the people that want to come back and keep working. You did a great job. So

518
00:42:32,660 --> 00:42:37,660
but yeah, I would if you can make it over there and help out in some way, it'll it'll change your life for sure.

519
00:42:38,660 --> 00:42:41,660
See that the suffering that was going on.

520
00:42:43,660 --> 00:42:45,660
Yeah, it touched me.

521
00:42:46,660 --> 00:42:49,660
Yeah, it's definitely a definitely cool experience.

522
00:42:50,660 --> 00:42:57,660
And I think it's awesome that we managed to recruit another human into the EMS family via the whole thing.

523
00:42:58,660 --> 00:42:59,660
Brad.

524
00:43:01,660 --> 00:43:08,660
Yeah, I guess I'll just mirror what Jason was saying. It's been it's been just a really, really rewarding experience.

525
00:43:09,660 --> 00:43:13,660
And it's being out here, it really feels like you can actually

526
00:43:14,660 --> 00:43:22,660
see this objective tangible benefit to the work in which you're doing. And it's just really, really awesome.

527
00:43:25,660 --> 00:43:28,660
Yeah, for some really, really appreciative people.

528
00:43:29,660 --> 00:43:42,660
You know, here in the US, we're used to working for people that expect good, good health care. They expect when they call 911 that they're going to have somebody show up that knows what they're doing. It can take them to a higher level of care. And those people are going to know what they're doing.

529
00:43:44,660 --> 00:43:50,660
They don't always have those expectations there. They'll take what they can get because they know their country's in a war.

530
00:43:51,660 --> 00:44:08,660
So over there, any type of little help, it just blew their minds, you know, that they would. Oh, you actually have medicines for us to help us out with the pain on this long transport that it blew their mind because they weren't expecting that. They were expecting to have an ex fix bouncing down the road in a lot of pain, you know.

531
00:44:09,660 --> 00:44:20,660
Yeah, there's this, I think there's this cultural tolerance of pain. I had my wisdom teeth removed out here and they were just like, take ibuprofen.

532
00:44:23,660 --> 00:44:26,660
Where did you have it done? I remember you talking about that.

